225988507	1086940	I really hope the studio gets to read this review!❤️\n\nThis is an incredibly fun game, honestly close to perfect. The level of freedom while still maintaining this degree of polish is genuinely impressive. This is my first turn-based combat game, yet it never felt boring. Despite the isometric perspective, the combat still has satisfying impact, and the visual effects are flashy enough to stay visually engaging throughout.\n\nHowever, I do have one major regret regarding the romance system. Every companion seems to set their romance flag far too early, and if you miss or reject it at that point, you never get another chance later on.\n\nI understand that for some players, appearance or first impressions are enough motivation to pursue a romance route immediately. But there are also players who only become emotionally invested after fully experiencing a character’s story and growth — players who enjoy slower-burn romances. For me, it’s not about collecting more scenes or unlocking intimate content earlier. The issue is that the game asks the player to accept physical intimacy almost at the same moment the romance begins, which can feel psychologically uncomfortable and rushed.\n\nI would have loved a system where, even if the early romance flag was missed, the player could still confirm the relationship later after learning the full depth of the character’s story. Alternatively, it could also work well if different companions had different romance pacing, flag timings, or numbers of opportunities depending on their personalities.\n\nI understand that implementing something like this likely comes with significant production and writing costs. I’m not asking for this game itself to be changed retroactively. I simply hope this is something the studio might consider for future titles that include romance systems.\n\nThank you!🥰	t	4121	koreana	2026-05-20 12:21:19	2026-05-23 11:13:15.05953
